Connects to every server in the manifest (same SSH path as audit), reads
the actual authorized_keys, and emits a complete new manifest in which
every installed key is accounted for: keys of known users found beyond
their grants extend that user's access; unknown keys become new users
with access to exactly the servers where they were found. The input
file is never modified — review the output, then adopt it as your
manifest. Any unreadable server aborts the run unless --partial is set.

Exit codes:
  0  manifest emitted
  1  a server could not be fully read and --partial was not given; nothing
     emitted
  3  tool error: bad flags, unloadable manifest, failed preflight, an existing
     --output without --force, interrupted

Usage:
  sshepherd adopt [flags]

Examples:
  # Bootstrap: append every installed key to a servers-only manifest
  sshepherd adopt --merge -o adopted.yaml

  # Regenerate from scratch, even if some servers are down
  sshepherd adopt --partial -o adopted.yaml

Flags:
      --config string                   path to the manifest (default "sshepherd.yaml")
      --force                           allow --output to overwrite an existing file
  -h, --help                            help for adopt
      --identity PATH                   private key PATH to authenticate with, in addition to ssh-agent (repeatable; ~/ expands from $HOME)
      --identity-passphrase KEY=FILE    KEY=FILE: passphrase file for one --identity key (repeatable; split at the first =)
      --identity-passphrase-file FILE   FILE holding the default passphrase for any encrypted --identity key
      --known-hosts string              path to known_hosts (strict host-key checking) (default "~/.ssh/known_hosts")
      --merge                           preserve the manifest text (comments intact) and append the additions instead of regenerating
  -o, --output string                   write the manifest to this file instead of stdout
      --parallel int                    max concurrent server reads (default 32)
      --partial                         proceed even if some servers cannot be read (their access will be missing from the output)

Global Flags:
      --color string   colorize output: auto, always, or never (default "auto")
  -q, --quiet          log errors only
  -v, --verbose        enable debug logging
